    {
      "id": "calculus-1-5:why-half-capacity",
      "type": "single_choice",
      "prompt": "Why is logistic growth fastest when N = K/2?",
      "points": 1,
      "objective_ids": [
        "calculus-1-5-objective-3"
      ],
      "options": [
        "Because the second derivative of N(t) is largest at t = 0.",
        "The rate rN(1 − N/K) is a downward parabola in N whose derivative r − 2rN/K is zero at N = K/2.",
        "Because half the cells stop dividing at that point.",
        "Logistic growth is fastest at N = K."
      ],
      "solution_spec": {
        "answer": 1
      },
      "feedback": {
        "hint": "Treat the rate as a function of N and maximize it.",
        "solution": "The growth rate is a quadratic in N with roots at 0 and K; its maximum is midway. On the N(t) curve this is the inflection point.",
        "lesson_ids": [
          "calculus-1-5"
        ],
        "misconception_ids": [
          "growth-fastest-at-capacity"
